# Line endings: LF everywhere, on every platform, forever.
#
# This project is developed and run on Linux, but is cloned on Windows too, where
# git's default core.autocrlf=true rewrites the working tree to CRLF on checkout.
# That flips the bytes of every text file, and mindex hashes `code.as_bytes()` —
# so every client (mindex-index, mindex-watch, the VS Code extension, the MCP
# index_files tool) reports permanent drift and reindexes the whole tree on every
# check, forever, with nothing erroring. See "Four clients, one working-tree view"
# in .claude/CLAUDE.md: what a client hashes must be exactly what /index posted.
#
# The rule below is repo-wide and unconditional: it covers every subdirectory,
# every tool, and every file type added in the future — including ones nobody has
# thought of yet. Do not add per-extension exceptions; if a new file type needs
# different handling, it is binary and belongs in the section at the bottom.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide text vs binary by content; `eol=lf` then pins the
# working-tree ending for everything it called text. It overrides core.autocrlf
# and core.eol on the developer's machine, which is the point — the setting must
# not be something each contributor has to get right.
*           text=auto eol=lf

# A Windows clone that predates this file still has CRLF in its working tree, and
# .gitattributes only applies at checkout. Normalize once, per clone:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git status          # expect no changes on a clone that was already LF
#
# Files executed by a shebang are the case where CRLF is not merely churn: the
# Docker image fails with a bare "exec /entrypoint.sh failed: No such file or
# directory", because the kernel is looking for the interpreter "/bin/sh\r".
# Covered by the rule above; kept explicit so the reason survives.
*.sh        text eol=lf
tools/hooks/* text eol=lf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text. `text=auto` already detects these,
# but declaring them removes the guess for content that can look textual.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.onnx      binary
*.safetensors binary
*.vsix      binary
